<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/2nd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Vercellae</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">101 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbrian-war">Cimbrian War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Vercellae was fought in 101 BCE on the Raudine Plain in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where Roman armies under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius">Gaius Marius</a> and Quintus Lutatius Catulus destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbri">Cimbri</a>. Coming a year after Marius's victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutones">Teutones</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aix-en-provence">Aquae Sextiae</a>, it ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbrian-war">Cimbrian War</a>, which had threatened Italy since the Roman disaster at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-vaucluse">Arausio</a> in 105 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-vercellae.jpg" alt="Battle of Vercellae" width="320" height="350" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giovanni Battista Tiepolo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tiepolo_Vercellae.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 113 BCE a migrating Germanic-Celtic alliance headed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbri">Cimbri</a> and the Teutons entered Noricum, and at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noreia">Noreia</a> overwhelmed a Roman army sent under the consul Gnaeus Papirius Carbo. They then moved into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and defeated Roman armies several times, most seriously at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-vaucluse">Arausio</a> in 105 BCE. There the quarrel between the patrician Quintus Servilius Caepio and the consul Gnaeus Mallius Maximus left the two Roman armies unwilling to support each other, and the defeat was on a scale not seen since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a>.</p><p>In 104 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius">Gaius Marius</a> against the invaders. In 102 BCE he defeated the Ambrones and Teutons near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aix-en-provence">Aquae Sextiae</a>, while his colleague Quintus Lutatius Catulus, charged with keeping the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbri">Cimbri</a> out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, retreated near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Tridentum</a> with his army intact. In July 101 BCE the Cimbri were moving west along the Po, and the armies of Marius and Catulus, joined near Placentia, were under Marius, now consul for the fifth time. Negotiations over land for the Cimbri came to nothing, and Marius displayed captured Teuton nobles to their envoys.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Marius agreed the time and place with the Cimbric king Boiorix, choosing the Raudine Plain near Vercellae. The site is traditionally placed near modern Vercelli, though some historians suggest the Polesine near Rovigo or Roddi in the province of Cuneo. The sources give the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> about 52,000 to 54,000 men and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbri">Cimbri</a> 120,000 to 180,000, figures modern historians treat with scepticism. On 30 July the Romans formed up facing west, so that the Cimbri fought with the morning sun in their eyes and the dust blowing toward them. Marius took the left wing, Catulus the centre and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> the cavalry on the right.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbri">Cimbri</a> advanced in a great square, their cavalry in front. Marius's wing marched into a dust cloud and missed the fighting, which took place around Catulus's centre. The Cimbric cavalry were thrown back by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>'s horsemen into their own infantry, and Catulus's legionaries advanced with javelins and swords while the other wings closed in. The summer heat added to the Cimbri's trouble. The rout ended against their wagons at the rear and became a massacre that stopped when the Cimbri began to surrender in numbers. Boiorix and his nobles died fighting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Marius claimed to have killed 100,000 and to have enslaved many thousands more, including women and children. Catulus quarrelled with Marius over credit, and the two held a joint triumph, with Marius receiving most of the praise. He then granted citizenship to his Italian allied troops without consulting the Senate. The war's end also began a growing rivalry between Marius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>, which would lead to the first of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s great civil wars.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Vercellae” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
